"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. The preoperative PSA was 3.9, and the surgical pathology revealed a Gleason 3+4 with tertiary pattern 5 (ISUP grade group 2), indicating a lower grade than the biopsy finding of Gleason 3+5 (ISUP grade group 4). Extraprostatic extension was present, and surgical margins were positive. Conversely, the absence of lymph node metastasis and seminal vesicle invasion are favorable findings.\n\nThe discrepancy between the biopsy and prostatectomy findings introduces uncertainty regarding the true extent of the disease. Missing postoperative PSA or subsequent follow-up data significantly widen the uncertainty surrounding recurrence risk. The current horizon suggests a potential recurrence within a range of 28 to 43 months. Information regarding postoperative PSA levels and their trajectory would be crucial in refining this prognosis. The fixed estimated horizon is 35.54 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 28.43–42.65 months; its coverage has not been validated."
